<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Tomis</h1><p class="st-meta">600 BCE – 72 BCE (approx.) · capital Tomis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Milesian colony on the western Black Sea coast, later famous as the place of Ovid's exile and today the Romanian port of Constanta.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tomis was settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/miletus">Miletus</a> on a peninsula of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> shore, in a region the Greeks called <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/scythia">Scythia</a> Minor, probably in the 6th century BCE. It began as a trading post beside the older colony of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/histria">Histria</a> and grew slowly into a city with its own territory, trading with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/getae">Getae</a> and other Thracian peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobruja">Dobruja</a> hinterland.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Like other West Pontic colonies, Tomis was a self-governing polis with a council and assembly and close ties to its mother city <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/miletus">Miletus</a>. Its economy rested on grain, fish and exchange with inland peoples, whose raids were a constant threat. It struck its own coins in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a>, and under Roman rule it headed the league (koinon) of the West Pontic Greek cities.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Roman general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-terentius-varro-lucullus">Marcus Terentius Varro Lucullus</a> brought the West Pontic cities under Roman control in 72-71 BCE. The poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a>, exiled there by Augustus in 8 CE, complained of its cold and its barbarous neighbours. Tomis later became the capital of the Roman province of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/scythia">Scythia</a> and is today Constanta.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 600 BCE – 72 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:47.76%;width:10.51%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>600 BCE – 72 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tomis</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Constanta</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-336&amp;focus=tomis" aria-label="Tomis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5366 614 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tomis lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5686 870L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5686 870h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5686 870h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tomis. <a href="/atlas?year=-336&amp;focus=tomis">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

